{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B042",
        "laureates": [
          "Michael Berry",
          "Yakir Aharonov"
        ],
        "citation": "for discovering observable geometric and topological phase effects in quantum mechanics"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B030",
    "B005"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B042 recognizes a completed conceptual transformation with a precise physical core: quantum phase can have observable global content, through topological and geometric effects. The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase arose separately, but are properly joined at the level of this common physical lesson, not presented as one historical episode. Both insights are long mature, experimentally tested, and broadly consequential across quantum physics and condensed matter. Credit is direct and defensible: Aharonov and Berry each originated one of the two explicitly delimited effects.",
    "case_against": "The configuration remains vulnerable if the common principle cannot be stated without concealing the distinct origins, theoretical settings, and experimental histories of the two effects. B030 is more compactly an invention-to-realization achievement, with unusually clear experimental maturity. B005 is also a compelling unified theoretical framework, although its intellectual antecedents and subsequent implementations make a three-person boundary less certain.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson judges the Aharonov-Bohm and Berry effects foundational and experimentally supported, but questions whether their different original settings permit a unified prize.",
        "response": "I accept this as the central objection and have retained the narrow formulation rather than asserting a single discovery. The defensible unity is the experimentally observable, nonlocal or geometric content of quantum phase; the citation must explicitly name effects in the plural and credit their separate originators. That is a physical, rather than merely rhetorical, prize boundary."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks are independently reproducible at 10^-18 precision, cleanly attributable to Katori and Ye, and prize-worthy even without a particular new-physics discovery.",
        "response": "I agree that B030 has strengthened as the clearest experimental alternative, especially because reproducibility shows that its central architecture is robust. But the present choice is not between sound and unsound work: B042 concerns a completed revision of the physical meaning of quantum phase, whereas B030 principally establishes a powerful measurement capability. I therefore retain B030 as an alternative and would keep any future citation confined to the clock architecture."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors the quantum spin Hall effect as a mature theory-to-experiment discovery of a new electronic state, while warning that its boundary with broader topological-insulator developments needs delimitation.",
        "response": "The theory-to-experiment line is strong, but the stated boundary problem is consequential. B042 has a more elementary and general conceptual mechanism, and its two credited names correspond exactly to the two effects named in the citation. I would not let broad technological promise outweigh this sharper completed conceptual achievement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether documentary review can confirm that a prize citation for observable geometric and topological phase effects preserves, rather than blurs, the separate priority and historical development of the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase.",
      "Whether the committee judges the unusually mature and compact optical-lattice-clock achievement to merit priority over a completed conceptual transformation.",
      "Whether the antecedents and later extensions of dynamical mean-field theory permit a sufficiently defensible three-person attribution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "Institutional proximity to Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson through the linked University of Gothenburg and Chalmers physics environment remains apparent; no specific collaboration or conflict concerning the preferred configuration is identified."
  }
}
